= Semantic Web — Technical =

== Layered architecture ==
A practical Semantic Web stack can be viewed as:

<pre>
IRI / Web identifiers
↓
RDF graph data model
↓
RDFS / SKOS / vocabularies
↓
OWL / rules / richer semantics
↓
SPARQL querying
↓
application logic / provenance / trust
</pre>

This is an explanatory stack rather than one mandatory deployment architecture.

== Decentralized semantics ==
Independent publishers can mint IRIs and vocabularies.

Interoperability depends on:
* vocabulary reuse;
* mappings;
* stable identifiers;
* explicit semantics;
* provenance.

Global naming alone does not create conceptual agreement.

== Entailment ==
RDF/RDFS/OWL define entailment regimes.

A query engine can:
* query explicit triples only;
* query a materialized closure;
* apply entailment during query.

Results depend on which regime is active.

== Open-world / monotonic reasoning ==
OWL/RDF reasoning is generally open-world and monotonic.

Adding knowledge can add consequences but does not usually retract previous logical consequences.

Validation and default reasoning therefore require separate mechanisms.

== Identity ==
IRI equality is syntactic identity of identifiers, not necessarily real-world identity of referents.

OWL identity assertions can link individuals but are strong semantic claims.

Entity resolution remains an application-level problem.

== Linked Data ==
Linked Data typically follows principles of:
* using Web identifiers;
* dereferenceable descriptions;
* RDF-compatible structured data;
* links to other identifiers.

Rich ontology reasoning is optional.

